A candidate's review scores it: `research_assess` records its vector and returns its score and
severity; never compute or delegate scoring. Score the impact were every premise to hold, the
note giving the rationale for each metric, the assumed deployment, the attacker's privileges and
the input states the chain needs. A condition the scenario needs is a premise on a chain flaw or
on a path, never a lower metric.

Choose a complete CVSS 4.0 base vector. Attack Complexity is the
defences an attacker must defeat; Attack Requirements are the conditions outside the attacker's
control that the scenario needs: a build option, configuration or deployment other than the
default, behaviour of the caller, client or deployment beyond supplying attacker input, or a race
or timing window the attacker must win, makes AT:P; a window the attacker can hold open at will
does not. Attack Vector, Privileges Required and the other exploitability
metrics come from what the attacker must hold or face before the first action, the
least-privileged actor that can drive every input state the chain needs, at the trust its path
reads it with (past the checks earlier stages make), a read of a stored state trusted as the
input of its least-privileged writer path, plus anything a later action
needs that no earlier action supplied; access or privilege gained during the scenario is never a
prerequisite. User Interaction is passive or active only when a victim's action is a step in the
scenario. Vulnerable-system impact is on the component the flaws are in; subsequent-system
impact applies only where the consequence lands in another system. Score only what the
candidate's premises establish: when an unlinked condition would raise a metric, keep the lower
value and record the higher reading as its own candidate, the same chain with that condition
linked as a premise. Never sum or inherit other candidates' scores.
